canonical-commutation-relations-poisson.symmetry_origin:
The canonical commutation relations arise from the fundamental symmetries of physical laws, particularly those related to translations in configuration space and the associated conservation laws (momentum conservation). In the Hamiltonian formulation of classical mechanics, they are derived from Hamilton's equations of motion and the definition of the Poisson bracket. The Poisson bracket itself is a consequence of the symplectic structure of phase space, which is preserved under canonical transformations, reflecting the underlying symmetries of the mechanical system. The relation 
\{r_i, p_j\} = \delta_{ij}
 is essentially a statement of the canonical nature of the coordinates and momenta.

